  "position": 1,
  "title": "Primes and prime factorisation",
  "summary": "Identify prime numbers and express positive integers as products of prime factors using index notation.",
  "outcomes": [
    "1.1"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 30,
  "objectives": [
    "Identify whether a positive integer is prime or composite.",
    "Write a positive integer as a product of prime factors in index notation."
  ],
  "content_status": "draft",
  "development_preview": true,
  "learning_material_state": "pending",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "prime-numbers",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Prime and composite numbers",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A factor of a positive integer divides it exactly, with no remainder. For example, the positive factors of "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "12"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " are "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "1,2,3,4,6,12"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ".\n\nA prime number is a whole number greater than 1 with exactly two positive factors: 1 and itself. A composite number is a whole number greater than 1 with more than two positive factors. The number 1 is neither prime nor composite because it has only one positive factor.\n\nThe first few prime numbers are "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "2,3,5,7,11,13,17,19"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ". The number 2 is the only even prime number."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "testing-primality",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: test whether numbers are prime",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Decide whether "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "91"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "97"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " are prime or composite."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "To test a number, check prime divisors up to its square root. Both square roots are less than "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "latex": "10"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", so check "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "latex": "2,3,5"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "latex": "7"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "91=7\\times13"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Therefore, 91 is composite."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The number 97 is not divisible by "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "latex": "2,3,5"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " or "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "latex": "7"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". Therefore, 97 is prime."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "For 91 we found a factor pair other than 1 and 91. For 97, no prime not exceeding its square root divides it."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "prime-factorisation",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Prime factorisation and index notation",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A prime factorisation writes a composite number as a product containing only prime factors. You may use repeated division or a factor tree. Keep splitting composite factors until every branch ends in a prime number.\n\nRepeated prime factors should be collected using index notation. For example, "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "2\\times2\\times2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " is written as "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "2^3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ". The final factorisation is usually written with prime bases in ascending order."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "factorise-360",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: express 360 as prime factors",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Express "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "360"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " as a product of prime factors in index notation."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Divide repeatedly by the smallest available prime:"
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "360=2\\times180=2\\times2\\times90=2\\times2\\times2\\times45"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Continue factorising "
            },
            {
              "type": "inline_math",
              "latex": "45"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ":"
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "45=3\\times15=3\\times3\\times5"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Collect equal factors using powers:"
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "360=2^3\\times3^2\\times5"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "All bases are prime, and 2 cubed times 3 squared times 5 equals 360."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "factorise-84-check",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Express "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "84"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " as a product of prime factors in index notation."
        }
      ],
      "response_type": "algebraic_expression"
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "common-mistakes",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common mistakes to avoid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Do not include 1 as a prime factor. Do not stop while a factor is still composite. For example, "
        },
        {
          "type": "inline_math",
          "latex": "84=4\\times21"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " is true but is not a prime factorisation because 4 and 21 are composite. Finally, check that multiplying your prime factors reconstructs the original number."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "lesson-summary",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson summary",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A prime number has exactly two positive factors. A composite number has more than two, while 1 is neither prime nor composite.\n\nTo obtain a prime factorisation, repeatedly split or divide the number until every factor is prime, collect repeated factors with index notation, and multiply the result to check it."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
